# Cloudflare Pages routing for the SPA.
#
# Without this file Pages auto-rewrites every unknown path to
# `/index.html` with HTTP 200 — which made the site indistinguishable
# from a wall of duplicate / soft-404 pages to Google. This file makes
# the distinction explicit:
#
#   - Known SPA routes (`/`, `/create`, `/profile`, `/token/<addr>`) are
#     rewritten to `/index.html` with HTTP 200 so React Router can take
#     over client-side.
#   - Everything else falls through to `/404.html` with HTTP 404, so
#     crawlers see a real 404 status and don't index the page as a
#     duplicate of the homepage. `404.html` is a copy of `index.html`
#     written by `scripts/post-build.mjs`, so the SPA shell still
#     renders the styled `NotFound` component.
#
# Static assets (favicons, OG image, /altfun-*.html docs, the audit
# PDFs, /whitepaper.pdf, hashed `/assets/*` bundles, …) are served from
# the static-asset layer before `_redirects` is consulted — no need to
# whitelist them here.
#
# Add a new entry whenever a new top-level SPA route is added in
# `src/app/routes.ts`. Routes nested deeper than the patterns below
# would need their own splat.

/                /index.html  200
/create          /index.html  200
/profile         /index.html  200
/token/*         /index.html  200

# `/audit.pdf` was the Phage Security review's path back when it was the
# only audit. Externally linked (X posts, docs.alt.fun, listing sites),
# so the old path keeps resolving. Must stay above the `/*` catch-all —
# Pages applies the top-most rule matching a source path.
/audit.pdf       /audit-phage-security.pdf  301

/*               /404.html    404
